The blocker is confined to the reference model’s intentionally opaque integer-to-float primitive, not the Python body. I’m applying the Kit contract’s trusted-primitive path: in the proof definition only, `intToF(I)` is reified as a fresh opaque Float term over the same complete integer domain. This preserves structural dependence on the exact input and avoids claiming any unproved IEEE value; LLVM execution will still use the untouched reference semantics.
